2. WHY A RE-RUN CANNOT BE THE RECEIPT
   The producer starts CityFace's HTTP handler on an ephemeral loopback port,
   drives 8 criteria over a real socket and a temp database, and stamps the
   port and the clock into the page. A second run carries different stamps by
   construction, so byte equality can never hold. The reproducible half is the
   pair as a function of its own report: facts from the json, stamps read back
   off the landed page.

3. READ-ONLY PROBE (system temp only, nothing written into the repo)
   Replaying the landed report through the producer's own
   verify()/render_html()/_document() triple and applying
   face_backhop.inject_text produced a 7988B page whose only difference from
   the landed page was the channel's own shelf stamp:
       data-back-bytes="55302"  (landed, recorded when R3288 injected)
       data-back-bytes="55645"  (today, measured from index.html now)
   The json half differed in exactly two fields: sizes.html 7300->7988 and
   sizes.json 5004->5197, i.e. the channel's second step restamped the report.

4. TWO DEFECTS FOUND IN THIS WINDOW'S OWN GATE
   (a) The landed report is checked out with CRLF while the producer writes LF
       (193 extra bytes = 193 lines). A json comparison can never match until
       the replay adopts the working tree's newline - and because the page's
       own AC-CFY1 row records the LF numbers the producer measured, the
       conversion has to happen AFTER the fixed-point loop, not inside it.
   (b) The channel restamps the json's recorded sizes with post-channel byte
       counts (face_backhop.patch_sizes). A replay that stops at the html
       comparison leaves the json half 193 bytes short and reads drift.

